      ":question/id": "3910d8c3-7517-5b80-9efc-a8b60965d481",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-34051",
      ":question/problem": "Find $k$, if $2{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2}=b$ and\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $k$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ 2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+k\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ k\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ k\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the final equation above, we can tell that $k=-3.$"
    },
