      ":question/id": "4eddae70-87f3-5f4b-9148-8e92a4a81ace",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-34570",
      ":question/problem": "Find $k$, if $3{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2}=0$ and \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $k$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 3\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+k\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ k\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ k\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the first components, we get \n\n$$\n3k=-6\\,\u27f9\\,k=-2.\n$$\n\nBut then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\\ne \\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there is no $k$ such that $3{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2}=0.$"
    },
