      ":question/id": "3695980d-a1ca-52c3-a275-096dc38f4d3e",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-34602",
      ":question/problem": "Find $k$, if $8{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2}=0$ and\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $k$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 8\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}+k\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ k\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-8\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ k\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}24 \\\\ -48 \\\\ 32\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the first components, we get \n\n$$\n-2k=24\\,\u27f9\\,k=-12.\n$$\n\nBut then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-12\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}24 \\\\ -36 \\\\ 48\\end{bmatrix}\\ne \\begin{bmatrix}24 \\\\ -48 \\\\ 32\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there is no $k$ such that \n $8{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2}=0.$"
    },
