      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-73923",
      ":question/problem": "Find $m$, if $5{a}_{1}+m{a}_{2}=0$ and \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $m$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{a}_{1}+m{a}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 5\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+m\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ m\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ m\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-30 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the first components, we get \n\n$$\n-5m=-30\\,\u27f9\\,m=6.\n$$\n\nBut then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-30 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix}\\ne \\begin{bmatrix}-30 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there is no $m$ such that $5{a}_{1}+m{a}_{2}=0.$"
    },
