      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-105156",
      ":question/problem": "Find $t$, if $2{a}_{1}+t{a}_{2}=0$ and\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $t$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}+t{a}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 2\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ t\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ t\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-16 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the first components, we get \n\n$$\n-4t=-16\\,\u27f9\\,t=4.\n$$\n\nBut then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-16 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}\\ne \\begin{bmatrix}-16 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there is no $t$ such that \n $2{a}_{1}+t{a}_{2}=0.$"
    },
